# Line endings, pinned for everyone (KAN-66).
#
# Without this file, line endings are decided by each developer's local
# `core.autocrlf`, which nobody on this project set deliberately. On a Windows
# checkout with `autocrlf=true`, git hands back CRLF while Prettier expects LF,
# so `npm run format:check` fails on every file in the repository regardless of
# what was changed — while the same command is green in CI, because the
# committed bytes were LF all along.
#
# That gap matters more than it looks. Step 6 of the execution plan tells every
# developer to run `format:check` before pushing; a check that always fails is a
# check everyone learns to ignore, and it is the one meant to catch real
# formatting problems. It also behaves differently per machine, so "passes for
# me" stops being evidence of anything.
#
# `text=auto` lets git classify each file, and `eol=lf` fixes the working-tree
# form on every platform. The index is already LF, so applying this changes no
# file content — only what lands on disk at checkout.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary formats, listed explicitly rather than left to detection. `text=auto`
# does classify these correctly today, but detection reads file contents, and a
# file that happens to look textual early on would be mangled by normalisation.
# Naming them removes that possibility for assets added later.
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.pdf binary
